    -- legacy 01a1514d-caa7-74ff-8fca-c4fcda87aba1 (open): Zenoh Profile 0 guest connecting out to a stock listener, ending on the stock peer's undeclaration
    -- The Zenoh Profile 0 demo has only ever exchanged its four samples between its own two nodes over the
    -- network service's loopback backend, where no NIC exists and no packet can be captured, and the stock
    -- eclipse-zenoh 1.0.0 peer has only been exercised from replayed bytes. The sel4-zenoh composition
    -- (generation 168) has no interface and grants one loopback destination, 127.0.0.1:7447, which the
    -- demo hard-codes. Nothing in the repository shows the guest opening a TCP connection out of the VM to
    -- a Zenoh peer it did not write, or says what such a connection may look like on the wire. A stock
    -- peer started in listen mode with multicast scouting and gossip off, on the host, is the smallest
    -- such peer, and QEMU user networking reaches a host listener at 10.0.2.2. Whether the connector
    -- reaches it, announces the lease the session sends keep-alives against, and delivers four samples the
    -- peer's own subscriber receives is unobserved. Once the four samples are delivered, the stock peer
    -- closes its subscription with an UndeclareSubscriber that names the subscriber by id alone, which the
    -- session refuses and the connector reports as a link failure; 01a1514d-8af5-7fa1-a4c2-70be8785fb2e
    -- takes that undeclaration into the session, and this exam's capture judge admits it in that one
    -- shape.
    (h.planned "01a1b49c-ac82-76a3-b7ef-a45087d33a6a"
      "The derived sel4-zenoh-external manifest is judged before QEMU starts, on 15 authority facts: generation 169, exactly the five named instances, exactly one network interface with the address, gateway, MAC and prefix above, exactly one destination row held by the publisher to 10.0.2.2 port 17447 as exact IPv4 TCP with exactly the rights connect, send and recv and no listen or resolver allowance, and the publisher as the only network application, a rightless client on the external backend that admits no peer. It refuses, on 30 mutations, each boundary: generation 168 and 170, an extra and a missing instance, no driver, no and two interfaces, another address, gateway, MAC and prefix, no, a second and a resolver destination row, port 17446 and 17448, the loopback address, a DNS name, UDP, a missing and an extra right, a listen limit, a resolver record, a destination held by the subscriber, a loopback backend, a publisher with rights, a listening publisher, a publisher admitting a peer, a subscriber application row and no application."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 guest connecting out to a stock listener, ending on the stock peer's undeclaration"
      "rehomed from a open item; unbound acceptance: cases-observed"
      h.noAcceptance) with { supersedes = { "01a1514d-caa7-74ff-8fca-c4fcda87aba1"; }; };
    (h.planned "01a1b49c-ac83-7091-ba42-854f64f6a914"
      "A listener record is read only through contracts/zenoh-external-receipt/v1. The decoder accepts 10 records, the honest one, one written by the listener's own writer, and eight on a boundary (keys of 1, 128 and 129 bytes, payloads of 11 and 12 bytes, and 2, 7 and 8 remote peers), and refuses 15: three and five samples, format version 2, another peer version, no and nine remote peers, a 130-byte and an empty key, a 13-byte payload, uppercase and odd-length hex, a 32-byte and a 34-byte attachment, and a field the contract does not name at the top and in a sample."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 guest connecting out to a stock listener, ending on the stock peer's undeclaration"
      "rehomed from a open item; unbound acceptance: controls-refused"
      h.noAcceptance) with { supersedes = { "01a1514d-caa7-74ff-8fca-c4fcda87aba1"; }; };
    (h.planned "01a1b49c-ac83-7091-ba42-855001627b33"
      "The listener is the pinned wheel, installed with --require-hashes and --no-deps into an environment that is rebuilt on every run and never reused, in peer mode with multicast scouting and gossip disabled, listening on 127.0.0.1 port 17447 only, with one subscriber on the demo key. It builds the environment with isolated interpreters (-I, and no PYTHON* or PIP_* variable) and pip --isolated, so a pip.conf in the caller's home changes nothing, runs the pinned interpreter with -E, updates its remote-peer count under one lock and floors it at one whenever a sample was delivered, and refuses to start if zenoh is imported from outside that environment, so a PYTHONPATH cannot substitute another package. It closes its session once all four samples have arrived and none has followed for three seconds, without waiting for the peer to disconnect (the connector keeps its link open until the peer closes it). It keeps a thread draining its output from start to exit, so a chatty child cannot block on a full pipe before it writes its record, and it reports ready within 30 seconds or the run is refused, whether the child exits or stays alive and silent. It counts at least one remote peer whenever a sample is delivered, and more when it polls them,  so a peer that connects, publishes and leaves between two polls is still seen. Its record names that endpoint and holds exactly four samples, each equal in key, payload and attachment to the sample the wire carried, in the order sent. A record that names another endpoint, saw no remote peer or more than eight, or differs in any one field of one sample is refused."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 guest connecting out to a stock listener, ending on the stock peer's undeclaration"
      "rehomed from a open item; unbound acceptance: cases-observed"
      h.noAcceptance) with { supersedes = { "01a1514d-caa7-74ff-8fca-c4fcda87aba1"; }; };
    (h.planned "01a1b49c-ac83-7091-ba42-85510e69ed45"
      "The capture holds exactly one TCP connection, from the guest to 10.0.2.2 port 17447, opened with one SYN and accepted with one SYN-ACK that acknowledges it, with only those two handshake packets (and identical retransmissions of them before any data, identical in window and in every TCP option but the timestamp) carrying a SYN flag, and no flag on them but SYN and ACK, and closed by FINs at the end of the bytes each side delivered. The judge refuses a capture with no connection, a connection the peer never accepts, a SYN-ACK that acknowledges one number too few or too many, a bare SYN from the peer, a SYN-ACK from the guest, or a SYN of either side after the connection carried data, a guest SYN retransmitted with a sequence number one over or one under, a SYN or SYN-ACK retransmitted with another window, segment size, window scale or SACK option, a malformed TCP option, and a fixed-format option of the wrong length (MSS other than 4 bytes, window scale other than 3, SACK-permitted other than 2, timestamps other than 10) on either side's handshake, a retransmitted SYN-ACK whose acknowledgement is one too few or one too many, a second connection from another source port, a SYN to port 17446 or 17448 or to another address, a connection whose only destination is port 17446 or 17448, a DNS query, a datagram to the scouting group, an ICMP echo, an ARP request for 10.0.2.99 or for 10.0.2.3, one claiming another sender address or another hardware address, one that is unicast, one naming a third party's target hardware address, one whose Ethernet source is not its sender hardware address, one that is not Ethernet and IPv4 and one that is neither a request nor a reply, a reply with a zero or a third party's target hardware address, to a third party's Ethernet address or to broadcast, a reset, a stream that never closes, a FIN one byte short of or past the data, a missing first or middle segment, and a capture that is empty, not a PCAP, truncated, or has a byte of its IPv4 header or of its TCP payload changed under a stale checksum. It accepts the same exchange re-segmented to 100 bytes and to 1 byte, with segments reordered, with one retransmitted, with either side's sequence number about to wrap, with keep-alives in the guest's stream, with no CLOSE from the guest, and with the peer undeclaring its subscriber by id alone after the last sample and then closing, as a stock peer does."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 guest connecting out to a stock listener, ending on the stock peer's undeclaration"
      "rehomed from a open item; unbound acceptance: controls-refused"
      h.noAcceptance) with { supersedes = { "01a1514d-caa7-74ff-8fca-c4fcda87aba1"; }; };
    (h.planned "01a1b49c-ac83-7091-ba42-855282ed8bd9"
      "The guest's stream is an INIT_SYN as a peer (wire version 9, a 1 to 16 byte ZID, optional size parameters, no extension, ending exactly at the batch boundary), an OPEN_SYN announcing exactly a 2000 ms lease and the initial sequence number the connector reported, then any number of one-byte keep-alives and exactly four reliable FRAMEs, each the canonical encoding by zenoh_wire of the demo key, the contract's CDR payload for its sample and the 33-byte attachment the publisher builds (sequence one over the sample number, source timestamp 1000 plus the sample number, and the one GID e0 to ef for every sample), with frame sequence numbers consecutive from the OPEN_SYN's initial number modulo 2**32 and each fitting 32 bits (an initial number of 0xFFFFFFFE or 0xFFFFFFFF wraps through zero, and a frame carrying the 33-bit value a non-wrapping count reaches is refused), then at most a session CLOSE of exactly two bytes, `23` and a reason of 0 to 7 (contracts/zenoh-profile/v1 closeConnectionToSelf is the highest the profile names). The batches are the bytes the connector reported sending. Capture times are read from the PCAP as exact integers. Each side's silence is bounded by the lease it announced (the guest's 2000 ms, the peer's from its OPEN_ACK; a gap of exactly the lease is admitted and one nanosecond-scale step over is refused), measured between the first packets of its consecutive batches after the INIT. The order across the two directions is judged from the packet that carried each batch: the INIT_ACK follows the whole INIT_SYN, the OPEN_SYN follows the whole INIT_ACK, the OPEN_ACK follows the whole OPEN_SYN, the peer declares a subscriber on the demo key (by name, or through the one alias it declared for that key) and still holds it, applying its declarations and undeclarations in message order (a subscriber declared and undeclared in one frame never held, an undeclaration must name the held subscriber, by id and key in the full form or by id alone in the form a stock peer sends behind the network QoS extension, and one before the last sample is refused), and only then does the first sample go, and a guest KEEP_ALIVE is carried only after the OPEN_ACK is complete, and the peer's stream ends in a Zenoh CLOSE ahead of its FIN, both after the last sample (the connector reads an end of stream with no CLOSE as an error before it can report success) (the connector fails on a close before it has sent all four), and the guest's SYN precedes the peer's SYN-ACK. The OPEN_SYN echoes the INIT_ACK's cookie byte for byte. Both INITs are parsed as the Profile 0 decoder reads them: wire version 9, a peer, a canonical 1 to 16 byte ZID, no extension, size parameters (when present) with the default resolution 10 and a non-zero batch size, a cookie of 1 to 64 bytes in an ACK, and nothing after. Both OPENs carry no extension and a non-zero lease. The peer's stream is an INIT_ACK, an OPEN_ACK (a non-zero lease and an initial sequence number and nothing after them), and then only reliable frames that carry no PUSH (the connector declared no subscriber, so one is refused by the session), numbered from the OPEN_ACK's initial sequence number and by one each (wrapping at 32 bits), whose every network message is parsed to the end of the batch (a declaration, a push or an OAM as the decoder takes them, with a trailing byte that starts no message refusing the batch), one-byte keep-alives and at most one two-byte close with a reason of 0 to 7, after which no batch of any kind. Each is parsed to its fields, not classified by its header. Every honest variant of the capture builds a different capture from every other and from the base, so the count of variants accepted is the count of cases exercised. The positive controls are pinned as the negative ones are: the checker requires exactly 46 honest captures and exactly 10 accepted listener records, so a deleted accepted case is caught as a deleted refusal is. The judge refuses a lease of 1999 and of 2001 ms, three and five frames, frames swapped, one byte changed on the wire only and on the wire and in the report, a sample one byte longer, a sequence number one over, an OPEN_SYN before the INIT_SYN, an INIT_SYN of three bytes, cut inside its ZID or its size parameters, with a byte after it, with an extension flag, of another wire version or as a client, an initial sequence number the report and the wire disagree on (999 against 0, 1 against 0, and 0 against 1), a two-byte keep-alive, a batch after the close, a guest close with bit 6 or an extension flag set, for the link only, with reason 8 or with reason 255, a batch kind the exam does not name from either side, an OPEN_SYN echoing another cookie, one byte short or with a byte added, with an empty cookie, an extension flag or a zero lease, an INIT with a zero batch size, resolution 9, 10's neighbour 11 or reserved resolution bits, or a ZID ending in a zero byte, an INIT_ACK with an empty cookie or a 65 byte cookie, a peer frame with an unsupported message byte, an unknown header, the unreliable form, a QoS extension on its header, a wildcard declaration or a byte after a whole message, an OAM that is not the link-state one, a peer that never declares a subscriber, a SYN-ACK recorded before the SYN, a SYN or SYN-ACK that also carries a FIN, URG or PSH flag, a peer close or FIN before the first sample or after three of the four, a peer stream that ends with no Zenoh CLOSE, a guest KEEP_ALIVE carried with the OPEN_SYN, a guest silent for 2000.001 ms or 3 s before its first sample, a peer silent for 10000.001 ms, a peer frame with a PUSH, an attachment sequence, timestamp or GID that differs from the publisher's by one (or that changes between samples) even when the report, the wire and the record agree on it, a data segment or a FIN without ACK or with URG, a segment acknowledging a byte the other side had not sent (0x70000000, or one past), the closing FINs and last ACK recorded before the SYN or between the SYN and the SYN-ACK, a handshake whose final ACK and first data segment both acknowledge nothing past the peer's ISN, a frame addressed to or sent from a MAC that is not the guest's, a peer subscriber on another key (through an alias or by name), a subscriber the peer declares and undeclares in one frame, undeclares before the first sample, undeclares by another id or key or undeclares twice, undeclares by id alone before the first sample, by an id it does not hold, twice, by id alone and then in full, in one frame with its declaration, without the QoS extension, with the extension flag and no extension, with a reserved header bit, with no id, with a trailing byte, with a QoS value over the bound, or in full behind the QoS extension, peer frames numbered one over or one under the OPEN_ACK's, repeated or skipped, and each step of the session out of turn, a peer that sends no INIT_ACK, a peer stream of three one-byte headers, an INIT_ACK of another wire version, from a client, with an extension flag, cut inside its ZID, with a cookie running past or short of the batch, an OPEN_ACK with no sequence number, with a byte after it or with a zero lease, a keep-alive with a byte after it or a flag set, a close of one or three bytes or with a reason of 8, a close with bit 6 set, a frame with no sequence number or no message, and a keep-alive, a frame or a second close after the peer's close."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 guest connecting out to a stock listener, ending on the stock peer's undeclaration"
      "rehomed from a open item; unbound acceptance: controls-refused"
      h.noAcceptance) with { supersedes = { "01a1514d-caa7-74ff-8fca-c4fcda87aba1"; }; };
    (h.planned "01a1b49c-ac83-7091-ba42-85530f601a87"
      "The connector's report has exactly the three denials the demo already reports (undeclared-endpoint, listen and scouting, once each), one session open as a connector with a 2000 ms lease, one declaration match on the demo key, four sent batches for samples 0 to 3 once each, and one close, in the order: denials, session open, declaration match, first sample, last sample, close last. It refuses a dropped or duplicated sent batch, a dropped denial, a denial reported twice, a denial the exam does not name (including undeclared-port, which the publisher checks and does not print), any connector line that is not exactly one of the named forms (including one that only contains a keyword, and a close line with an extra field), a line from the subscriber, a close before the last sample, a match before the session, and sent batches reported before the session, between the session and the match, with samples 2 and 3 swapped, or with sample 0 last."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 guest connecting out to a stock listener, ending on the stock peer's undeclaration"
      "rehomed from a open item; unbound acceptance: controls-refused"
